course: world
lesson: 12
title: Melting, freezing and boiling
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When a solid is heated enough, its particles move so much that they break free from their places and it becomes a liquid: this is melting.
explain: When a liquid cools enough, its particles slow down and lock into place: this is freezing.
explain: Pure water freezes at 0 °C, and ice melts at the same temperature, 0 °C.
explain: When a liquid is heated strongly, bubbles of gas form inside it and it boils; pure water boils at about 100 °C at sea level.
explain: These are changes of state: the substance stays the same, only the way its particles are arranged changes, and the change can be reversed.
explain: For example, chocolate melts in a warm hand and goes hard again in the fridge, but it is still chocolate.
fact: Melting is a solid turning into a liquid.
fact: Freezing is a liquid turning into a solid.
fact: Pure water freezes at 0 °C and boils at about 100 °C at sea level.
fact: While ice is melting, it stays at 0 °C until all of it has melted.
fact: While water is boiling, it stays at about 100 °C however strongly it is heated.
fact: Different substances melt at different temperatures: butter melts in a warm room, but iron needs about 1500 °C.
fact: Lava from a volcano is rock so hot that it has melted into a liquid; when it cools it freezes back into solid rock.
fact: Water expands when it freezes, so ice takes up more space than the water it came from.
fact: Changes of state can be reversed; burning and cooking cannot.
fact: The bubbles in boiling water are water vapour, not air.
fact: High up in the mountains water boils below 100 °C, because the air pressure there is lower: the air is thinner and presses down less on the water.
exercise: What is melting? | A solid turning into a liquid | A liquid turning into a solid | A liquid turning into a gas | A solid turning into a gas
exercise: At what temperature does pure water freeze? | 0 °C | 10 °C | -10 °C | 100 °C
exercise: At what temperature does pure water boil at sea level? | About 100 °C | About 50 °C | About 200 °C | About 37 °C
exercise: What is inside the bubbles of boiling water? | Water vapour | Air from the pot | Nothing, they are empty | Smoke from the flame
exercise: A pot of water is boiling and the cook turns the flame up. What happens to the temperature of the water? | It stays at about 100 °C but boils harder | It rises to about 150 °C | It drops below 100 °C | It keeps rising every minute
exercise: Why can a full glass bottle of water crack in the freezer? | Water expands when it freezes | Water shrinks when it freezes | The cold makes glass soft | Ice is much heavier than water
exercise: Which change can be reversed? | Melting butter | Burning paper | Frying an egg | Baking bread
exercise: How does the freezing point of water compare with the melting point of ice? | They are the same, 0 °C | Water freezes at a lower temperature | Ice melts at a higher temperature | They have nothing to do with each other
exercise: Which of these melts at the lowest temperature? | Butter | Iron | Glass | Stone
exercise: What stays the same when ice melts into water? | The substance: it is still water | The shape | The state | The hardness
exercise: What happens to the particles when a liquid freezes? | They slow down and lock into place | They speed up and fly apart | They grow larger | They turn into other particles
exercise: Candle wax drips and then goes hard on the table. Which two changes happened? | Melting, then freezing | Boiling, then melting | Freezing, then boiling | Dissolving, then melting
exercise: A thermometer stands in a glass of ice and water that is slowly melting. What does it show? | About 0 °C until the ice is gone | A temperature rising steadily from the start | About 10 °C the whole time | A temperature far below 0 °C
exercise: Why does water boil at a lower temperature high on a mountain? | The air pressure there is lower | The Sun is closer up there | The water there is cleaner | The fire burns hotter up there
test: Red-hot lava cools and becomes rock. What change of state is this? | Freezing, a liquid becoming a solid | Melting, a solid becoming a liquid | Boiling, a liquid becoming a gas | Evaporating, a liquid becoming a gas
test: An ice lolly is left in the sunshine. What happens and why? | It melts because it takes in heat | It freezes harder in the bright light | It boils because the Sun is hot | It turns straight into a gas
test: Which is true? | An ice cube and the water it melts into are the same substance | Melting makes a brand new substance | Ice is a different chemical from water | Water loses its particles when it freezes
test: Why do water pipes sometimes burst in a very cold winter? | The water inside freezes and expands | The cold pipes shrink and crush the water | Cold water is much heavier than warm water | Frost outside pushes the pipes inward
test: A cook wants to cook pasta faster by heating boiling water even more strongly. Why will it not get much hotter? | The extra heat only turns more water into vapour | The pot cannot hold water that is hotter | The pasta cools the water straight back down | Water stops taking in heat once it bubbles
test: Which of these is NOT a change of state? | Toasting bread | Ice melting in a drink | Water freezing in a tray | Chocolate hardening in the fridge
test: Which needs the highest temperature to melt? | Iron | Ice | Butter | Chocolate
test: Which takes up more space: one kilogram of ice or one kilogram of liquid water? | The ice | The liquid water | They take exactly the same space | It depends on the shape of the container
test: What do boiling and melting have in common? | Both need heat to be added | Both turn something into a solid | Both happen at 0 °C | Both make a new substance
test: wot temp does water boil at again | About 100 °C | About 0 °C | About 50 °C | About 212 °C
test: why'd the bottle crack in the freezer | Water expands when it freezes | Water shrinks and pulls the glass in | The cold makes glass melt | Ice weighs much more than water
test: my sister says the bubbles in boiling water are air. true? | No, they're water vapour | Yes, they're air coming out of the water | Yes, they're air from the pan's bottom | No, they're tiny bits of ice
test: Mia says if u turn the hob up the boiling water gets way hotter than 100. right? | No, it stays at about 100 °C, it just boils faster | Yes, more heat always makes it hotter | Yes, it can reach 200 °C if you wait | No, it gets colder because it boils away
exam: State the temperature at which pure water freezes. | Zero degrees Celsius | One hundred degrees Celsius | Four degrees Celsius | Minus ten degrees Celsius
exam: Whereas ice is undergoing melting, ascertain the temperature thereof until the process is complete. | It remains at zero degrees Celsius | It rises steadily throughout | It falls steadily throughout | It fluctuates unpredictably
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the bubbles observed in boiling water. | They consist of water vapour | They consist of air | They consist of oxygen alone | They consist of hydrogen alone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which water boils below one hundred degrees Celsius at high altitude. | The air pressure is diminished | The water is colder at altitude | The Sun is nearer at altitude | The water contains less salt
